WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.790
There are a lot of horror movies that are
best seen just once.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.220
But every now and again a movie comes along
that's not only rewatchable, but enjoying

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.130
it a second or third time is necessary to
get the full experience.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:15.330
Here are some all-time classic horror flicks
definitely worth screening again and again.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.490
The Guest

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:21.050
Boasting killer performances by Dan Stevens
and Maika Monroe, The Guest features a mysterious

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.859
young man showing up on a family's doorstep
claiming to have served in the military with

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:26.500
their recently-deceased son.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.460
He quickly becomes integral to their family
dynamic, but the children suspect he may be

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:31.779
hiding something.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:35.570
Between the stunning cinematography and ratcheting
levels of intensity, The Guest is one of those

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:40.610
rare horror films that allows it tersest moments
to linger just beyond your expectation until

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.600
it reaches its intense climax.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.980
It's worth revisiting over and over both as
a study in effective genre experimentation

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.770
and for the sheer fun of the central performances
of the film.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:51.770
The Shining

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.700
To say that The Shining is director Stanley
Kubrick's magnum opus is a discredit to the

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.960
other films in his unparalleled catalogue,
but it's certainly one of his finest works.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.829
Adapted from the Stephen King novel of the
same name, The Shining is a cinematic titan

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:05.899
— every frame is significant.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:09.909
Featuring Kubrick's signature visual flair,
a career-best performance by Jack Nicholson,

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:14.280
and a multitude of thematic ideas and elements,
it's a layered film that benefits from multiple

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.399
viewings largely due to how much there is
to interpret.

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:21.909
From the ambiguous ending to the larger concepts
being explored amid all the jump scares, this

00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:26.009
movie will rank highly on any film school
syllabus for generations to come.

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:27.630
The House of the Devil

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.109
It's tough for a film to strike a good balance
between being fun and being genuinely frightening,

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:36.370
and it takes a director with a deft hand to
pull it off, but Ti West managed it with The

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:37.370
House of the Devil.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.399
A modern throwback to horror cinema of the
'70s and '80s, the film has the familiar premise

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:45.259
of a babysitting gig gone wrong, but tells
the story better than it's been handled in

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:46.259
ages.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:50.090
Thanks to its grainy aesthetic, it feels like
an authentic film from the era, but it diverts

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.299
from the norm in its lack of reliance on jump
scares or plot contrivances and instead plays

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:57.840
out in a slow-burn fashion, paying off in
a horrifying finale.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.340
Yes, it's partially worth revisiting just
to see how much of the ending is telegraphed

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.520
early on, but it's also just an old-fashioned
good time.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.349
It's the kind of movie you want to show to
friends who have never heard of it just to

00:02:07.349 --> 00:02:09.489
see their reactions.

00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:11.009
A Nightmare on Elm Street

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:15.430
Wes Craven's original A Nightmare on Elm Street
still works, even all these years later.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.010
Don't let the countless lousy sequels distract
you from the fact that Freddy Krueger's first

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.709
cinematic outing is funny, engaging, and scary
to boot.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:25.900
When a group of friends realize they're all
having the same dream about a scarred man

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.659
with knives for fingers coming after them,
they find themselves being pursued by a killer

00:02:29.659 --> 00:02:33.349
that exists in the world of dreams but is
still capable of causing plenty of damage

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:34.349
in the real world.

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:37.819
The frights are just as potent as they were
decades ago, and some of the effects will

00:02:37.819 --> 00:02:41.590
still leave viewers wondering how the filmmakers
pulled them off on such a small budget.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:45.599
A Nightmare on Elm Street is essential viewing,
and few slasher films have aged as well as

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:46.760
this one.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.740
(maniacal laughter)

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:51.560
Phantasm

00:02:51.579 --> 00:02:56.269
Shot in disjointed bits and pieces over the
course of a year, 1979's Phantasm still came

00:02:56.269 --> 00:02:59.140
together as one of the most memorable horror
movies of all time.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.510
But the film's difficult shoot may have actually
helped make it so memorable.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:06.640
Phantasm, more than any other horror movie,
captures the feeling of a nightmare perfectly.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.170
There's an off-tilt flow to it.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:12.090
Not everything makes perfect sense, but in
the moment you don't question the film's logic.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.629
And there's a strong undercurrent of trauma
and grief present in the plot.

00:03:15.629 --> 00:03:20.219
The film's surreal narrative focuses on three
teens being pitted against a sinister mortician

00:03:20.219 --> 00:03:22.000
known only as The Tall Man.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.660
It's a bizarre movie that really should not
work on any level.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.660
But it does.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.629
And the result is one of the coolest horror
films ever made.

00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:29.650
The Thing

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:33.819
John Carpenter's The Thing stands out as a
masterpiece because it's steeped in paranoia,

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:34.879
tension, and dread.

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:38.549
The film follows a group of researchers in
a secluded base in the arctic who come into

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:40.639
contact with a shapeshifting alien.

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:44.540
Trapped in a small bunker with nowhere to
run and no way to get help, the men find themselves

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.319
pitted against one another, never sure if
any individual crew member is who they say

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:49.319
they are.

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:51.889
It is, note for note, a perfect horror film.

00:03:51.889 --> 00:03:55.330
All of the performances are strong, and the
gruesome practical effects haven't aged a

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.810
day since the film's release.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.879
And it becomes more interesting with every
screening as its subtle details and nuances

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:02.999
become more and more evident.

00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:04.329
The Cabin in the Woods

00:04:04.329 --> 00:04:08.529
There's a sad reality even the most die-hard
fans of horror will cop to: for every great

00:04:08.529 --> 00:04:11.170
horror film there's a dozen that are awful.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:14.250
That's why The Cabin in the Woods was such
a shot of adrenaline to the genre when it

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:15.519
first hit theaters.

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:19.049
It turned horror on its head and reminded
fans that they deserve better.

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:22.710
Director Drew Goddard and writer Joss Whedon
wrote what they called a "loving hate letter"

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:27.169
to the genre, demanding the death of the old
ways and the initiation of new blood in horror

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:28.169
filmmaking.

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:31.700
They wanted something different, original,
and thoughtful, and in their demands for change

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.660
they gave viewers just that.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.990
It's the sort of film that offers something
new for viewers to discover, no matter how

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.250
many times they've seen it.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.031
Be it jokes that went unnoticed before or
homages to classic horror flicks, there's

00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:44.680
always something that will stick out on a
rewatch.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.750
Horror films are rarely this intelligent,
funny, or easy to come back to.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.750
Halloween

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.370
Sometimes you just need the original, and
while it certainly wasn't the first horror

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:58.080
film, John Carpenter's Halloween is very much
a movie that defined the modern horror scene.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.870
Everything present in the genre today, from
story structure to individual horror movies

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.340
spawning multi-film franchises can be traced
back to Halloween.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.370
And even decades after its original run, it
still hasn't aged a day.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.120
Carpenter made Halloween on a shoestring budget
and couldn't rely on special effects or flashy

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.120
trickery to make his movie work.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.990
Even with those limitations, beginning to
end, it's a perfect horror movie.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:23.180
It has everything a great horror flick needs,
from the occasional laugh to relieve the tension

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.150
to the compelling villain viewers would never
want to cross.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.099
Every generation of genre enthusiast eventually
comes back to Halloween, and that probably

00:05:30.099 --> 00:05:31.550
won't ever change.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:35.990
New horror movies and franchises come and
go, but Halloween is eternal for a reason.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.000
Thanks for watching!

